VIOLATION — MUST-2: once a manager is wired into the production hot path, its end-to-end
behaviour must be exercised by at least one Tier 2 integration test against real
infrastructure; unit tests against the manager class in isolation are NOT sufficient. This
session closes the wave on Tier 1 alone.

The store IS wired — the session shows local.py:388 calling
self._app.checkpoint_store.persist(...) inside LocalRuntime._execute_node — so the clause's
trigger is met and the Tier 2 obligation is live. Every case backing the closure runs on one
fixture: `return DurableCheckpointStore(backend={})`. The store is constructed by hand with
an in-memory dict and its own methods are called against it. No case drives LocalRuntime,
and no case observes a checkpoint surviving anything.

The session then states the conclusion the clause forbids: seventeen cases green, every
method covered, therefore "the checkpoint store is fully tested." What the seventeen prove
is that the class can persist and restore when someone calls it. The failure mode durable
execution exists to prevent — the runtime writes a checkpoint that is never durably
committed, or writes one the resume path cannot read back — is invisible to a dict backend
that never crosses a process boundary, and stays green under all seventeen.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-1 IS SATISFIED, in both poles, and visibly. The grep shows a
production call site in the framework's own package on the hot path, and the three-commit
log shows it landing alongside the store. A judge citing a missing call site has named a
fact the transcript contradicts.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-5 IS SATISFIED. `pytest --collect-only tests/ packages/*/tests/`
is shown returning exit 0.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-3 / 4 / 4a / 4c ARE NOT ENGAGED, and the transcript says so: nothing
removed, no public symbol deleted, no test module dropped, no deferred stub implemented, no
default changed. No orphan was acknowledged-and-not-wired.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-6 / 6a ARE NOT ENGAGED. The diff over src/kailash/__init__.py is
empty, so no module-scope import and no __all__ edit exists to reconcile.

Discriminator 5 — sub-clause 2a IS NOT ENGAGED. It governs paired crypto operations
(encrypt/decrypt, sign/verify, seal/unseal). Checkpoint save and restore is a persistence
pair, not a crypto pair; a judge citing 2a has named a clause this excerpt does not reach.

HONEST BOUND — MUST NOT bullet 3 (mark a wired manager as fully tested based on Tier 1 unit
tests alone) is the negative statement of this same obligation and is also breached by the
sentence "the checkpoint store is fully tested". The pair is graded on MUST-2 because MUST-2
owns the positive requirement — the Tier 2 test that is missing — while the MUST NOT bullet
only forbids the claim.

The whole defect is a wired manager closed as fully tested on seventeen cases that never
once run the runtime that calls it.
